window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["dsa"]={"atlas": [[null, ["Hi, I'm Buddy, and I'll walk you through this whole sheet the way a good teacher would at a whiteboard. Think of this first section as the map of the territory. Before we learn any single technique, I want you to see how all the pieces of data structures and algorithms fit together, because in an interview the hardest part is usually not writing the code. It is recognising which tool the problem is asking for."]], ["complete-dsa-topic-map", ["Look at the topic atlas. Everything in coding interviews falls into six areas. First, foundations: complexity, invariants, recursion and testing. These are the habits that make every other topic work. Second, sequences and lookup: arrays, hashing, prefix sums, two pointers, sliding windows, sorting, binary search and intervals. This is where most medium questions live. Third, data structures: linked lists, stacks and queues, heaps, trees and tries, union find, and range structures like Fenwick and segment trees. Fourth, problem solving paradigms: divide and conquer, greedy, backtracking, dynamic programming, and a few advanced tricks like meet in the middle. Fifth, graphs: traversal, topological order, shortest paths, spanning trees, and the more advanced connectivity algorithms. And sixth, strings, bits and maths. Each chip on the map is a link, so whenever you feel lost later, come back here and jump."]], ["route-from-the-question-to-a-pattern", ["This diagram is the single most useful habit I can give you. Start by reading the constraints and asking: what shape of answer do they want? If they want a position, a pair or a rank, ask whether the data is sorted or monotone. If yes, binary search or two pointers. If not, a hash map, or sort first and then scan. If they want something about a contiguous range, ask whether window validity is monotone, meaning that if a window is valid, every smaller window inside it is also valid. If so, a sliding window works. If not, for example when values can be negative or you need an exact sum, switch to prefix sums with a hash map. Range aggregates over many queries point to range structures. Next greater or window maximum points to a monotonic stack or deque. Enumerating every arrangement means backtracking. Optimising or counting over choices with repeated states means dynamic programming, and without repeated states, try to prove a greedy choice. Reachability or dependencies mean graphs, and pattern matching means string tools. Before you code, always say the invariant out loud, why the next step is safe, and check the complexity against the constraints."]], ["range-query-route", ["Range queries deserve their own little decision tree, because the right structure depends on two questions. First, do the values change? If they never change, prefix sums answer range sums in constant time, and a sparse table answers range minimum or maximum in constant time, because those operations are idempotent, meaning overlapping pieces don't double count. If you have many range updates and only read at the end, use a difference array. If values change one point at a time, use a Fenwick tree for sums and frequencies, or a segment tree when you need a general combine like minimum or greatest common divisor. If whole ranges are updated, you need a segment tree with lazy propagation. And if you are allowed to answer the queries in any order, offline techniques like Mo's algorithm or sort and sweep can be simpler."]], ["coverage-map-where-each-family-lives", ["This coverage table is just a directory. It tells you which section of the sheet holds each family: arrays and search, linear structures, trees, graphs, choices and optimisation, strings and maths, and range data structures. Use the section numbers to jump straight to the templates when you are revising one family."]], ["advanced-pattern-triggers-to-complete-the-map", ["These are the advanced triggers, the signals that you need something beyond the standard toolkit. If you see range queries plus updates, think Fenwick or segment tree. If values are huge but you only care about their order, compress them to ranks first. If many patterns must be found in one text, that is Aho Corasick: a trie of patterns with failure links, scanning the text once. Many substring comparisons suggest a suffix array. Shortest paths with special structure have special tools: relax in topological order on a DAG, use a zero one BFS when weights are only zero or one, or bidirectional search when you know both endpoints. Matching and capacity problems are network flow. When the input has around forty items and two to the forty is too big, split it into two halves of twenty, enumerate each half, and combine them. That is meet in the middle. You won't see these often at entry level, but naming the right family earns real credit."]], ["one-reusable-solve-flow", ["Here is a solve flow you can reuse on every problem. Constraints first, then the shape of the answer, then the invariant or the DP state, then the data structure or recurrence, then a short proof of why it works, then the complexity, and finally a dry run on an edge case. And always name the brute force first. Every optimisation you make should remove something specific: a repeated scan, a repeated state, or an unnecessary choice. If you can say which one you removed, you understand your solution."]]], "play": [[null, ["Now let's talk about the interview itself, because technique without a plan falls apart under pressure."]], ["45-min-l3-plan", ["A forty five minute coding round has a rhythm. Spend the first five minutes clarifying: how big is the input, is it sorted, can there be negatives or duplicates, what if it's empty, and what exactly should I return. From minute five to fifteen, describe the brute force, state its Big O, then optimise, and name the pattern out loud. Interviewers love hearing you say \"this is a sliding window because the validity is monotone\". From fifteen to thirty five, write clean code with small helper functions and meaningful names. In the last ten minutes, dry run your code on the example and on one edge case, and state the time and space complexity. If you run out of time, a clear plan with partial code beats rushed code that doesn't work."]], ["constraint-target-complexity", ["This table lets you read the intended solution straight from the constraints. Modern judges and interviewers assume roughly a hundred million simple operations per second. So if n is at most ten, factorial time is fine, meaning permutations or brute force backtracking. Up to twenty, two to the n is fine: subsets or bitmask DP. Up to five hundred, n cubed works, like Floyd Warshall or interval DP. Up to two thousand, n squared, like a two dimensional DP. Around a hundred thousand, you need n log n: sorting, heaps, binary search. Up to a million, linear time: two pointers, hashing, sliding windows. And if n is a billion, you can't even read every value, so the answer must be logarithmic: binary search on the answer or a formula. Get into the habit of glancing at n before anything else."]], ["advanced-patterns-use-the-atlas-triggers", ["This card reminds you where the advanced families live, and lists the classic mistakes that cost people offers. Off by one in binary search, from mixing the \"low less than or equal to high\" and the \"low less than high\" styles. Mutating a list while iterating over it. Python's shared default argument, where a list default is created once and reused across calls. Forgetting the visited set in a graph. Integer overflow when computing the middle index in Java or C plus plus, which is why you write low plus half the difference. And in backtracking, appending the path itself instead of a copy, so every saved answer ends up empty. Each of these is easy to avoid once you've named it."]], ["complexity-analysis-toolkit", ["Let's build some intuition for analysing complexity. For divide and conquer, the master theorem compares the work done at each level with the number of subproblems. Two halves with constant extra work, like a tree traversal, is linear. Two halves with linear extra work, like merge sort, is n log n. One half with constant work, like binary search, is log n. And the naive Fibonacci recursion, which calls itself twice on almost the same size, is exponential, roughly the golden ratio to the power n. Then there's amortised analysis. A dynamic array occasionally doubles, which is expensive, but averaged over all appends each one costs constant time. A monotonic stack is linear overall because every element is pushed once and popped at most once. Recursion uses space equal to its maximum depth. Backtracking costs roughly the branching factor to the power of the depth. Graph traversals are V plus E. And watch out for hidden costs: building a string by concatenation in a loop is quadratic, so collect pieces in a list and join once."]]], "kw": [[null, ["This next section is about pattern recognition through keywords. It's a little like learning to read the problem statement as a list of hints."]], ["keyword-pattern", ["Here's the keyword table, and I'd like you to almost memorise it. Sorted array with a pair or target: two pointers or binary search. Longest or shortest contiguous subarray or substring: sliding window, but only when values are positive. Subarray sum equals k with negatives allowed: prefix sums with a hash map seeded with zero mapped to one. Top k, k-th largest or a running median: heaps. Next greater or smaller element, spans and histograms: monotonic stack. The minimum value that satisfies a condition: binary search on the answer. All combinations, permutations or subsets: backtracking. Shortest path in an unweighted graph or grid: BFS. Weighted shortest path: Dijkstra for non negative weights, Bellman Ford when weights can be negative. Dependencies and ordering: topological sort. Groups that merge over time: union find. Overlapping intervals: sort by start and merge. Prefixes of words: a trie. Counting ways or minimising cost over choices: dynamic programming. Cycle or middle of a linked list: fast and slow pointers. And maximum XOR of a pair: a binary trie."]], ["decision-flow", ["The decision flow turns that table into a sequence of questions. Is the input sorted, or can you sort it without losing anything? Then two pointers or binary search. Is the question about a contiguous range? With positives, a sliding window; with negatives, prefix sums. Is it a graph or a grid? Unweighted, BFS or DFS; weighted, Dijkstra. Is it about choices where you want the optimum or a count? If subproblems overlap, dynamic programming; if not, greedy, but only after you can argue why the greedy choice is safe. And if they want you to list everything, backtracking. Walk through these questions in order, and nine times out of ten you'll land on the right family."]]], "trees": [[null, ["This section has seventeen small decision trees, one per topic. Each is a ladder of yes or no questions. You read from the top, and the first question you answer yes to gives you the technique. Let me take you through them one by one."]], ["which-array-pattern-when", ["For arrays, first ask whether the input is sorted or whether sorting is allowed. If so, two pointers solve pair sums, three sums, merging and intersections. If not, is the question about a contiguous subarray? Maximum sum is Kadane's algorithm, and for maximum product you must track the minimum too, because a negative times a negative flips into the largest value. A sum equal to k with negatives needs prefix sums and a hash map, while with all positives a sliding window is enough. If the task is rearranging in place, few distinct values suggest the Dutch national flag partition, and rotations suggest the reversal trick. If you need an element that appears more than n over k times, Boyer Moore voting keeps k minus one candidates. And counting pairs where i is less than j with some condition, like inversions, is done during merge sort."]], ["which-window", ["Sliding windows have their own ladder. First, is it really about a contiguous range? If not, it's not a window at all. If the window size is fixed, add the new element on the right and drop the one that falls off the left. If the size isn't fixed, ask whether validity is monotone. If a negative number can make a bad window good again, the window logic breaks, and you should switch to prefix sums. If validity is monotone, the question tells you the variant. For the longest window, expand the right edge and shrink while invalid. For the shortest, expand until valid and then shrink while it stays valid, recording as you go. To count windows with at most k of something, add the window length at every step. And to count exactly k, use a lovely trick: at most k minus at most k minus one."]], ["which-binary-search", ["Binary search is broader than finding a number in a sorted array. If the array is sorted, use lower bound and upper bound to find first and last occurrences, floors and ceilings. If it's rotated, at every midpoint one half is still sorted, so check which half and whether the target lies inside it. With duplicates, when the low, middle and high values are equal you can't tell, so shrink both ends by one. If there's no sorting but there is a slope, like finding a peak, compare the middle with its right neighbour. The most powerful variant is binary search on the answer. When the question asks for the minimum of a maximum, like the smallest capacity that ships everything in d days, you binary search over possible answers and test each one with a greedy feasibility check. For a median of two sorted arrays, partition the smaller array. And for matrices, either flatten the index or walk a staircase from the top right corner."]], ["which-stack-queue-tool", ["Stack and queue problems also have a ladder. Need the nearest bigger or smaller element to the left or right? That's a monotonic stack. Need the sum of minimums over every subarray? Use the contribution technique: each element is the minimum for a number of subarrays equal to its distance to the previous smaller times its distance to the next smaller, being strict on one side so ties aren't double counted. Sliding window maximum is a monotonic deque of indices. Largest rectangle under bars is a monotonic stack where you compute the area when a bar is popped. Matching brackets and undo operations are plain stacks. And caches: LRU uses a hash map plus a doubly linked list, while LFU adds frequency buckets and tracks the minimum frequency."]], ["which-linked-list-primitive", ["Linked list questions reuse a handful of primitives. If the head might change, as in deletion, merging or partitioning, start with a dummy node so you never special case the head. If you need a position without knowing the length, use fast and slow pointers: when the fast pointer reaches the end, the slow one is at the middle. For the n-th node from the end, move one pointer n steps ahead first and then move both. For cycles, Floyd's algorithm: if the pointers meet, there's a cycle, and to find where it starts, reset one pointer to the head and move both one step at a time until they meet again. Reversal handles palindromes and k-group problems. Merging two sorted lists is the building block for sorting a list. And to find where two lists intersect, walk each pointer through its own list and then the other one; they meet at the intersection because both travel the same total distance."]], ["which-binary-tree-technique", ["For binary trees, the first question is whether the answer at a node depends on both subtrees. Height, diameter, maximum path sum and the balanced check all do, so you use post order: compute the children first, return information upward. If you need something per level, like a zigzag order or the right view, do breadth first search processing one level at a time. Per column views need BFS with a column index. If you need to move upward from a target node, like finding all nodes at distance k, build a parent map and then BFS outward. Lowest common ancestor returns whichever side is non null, and if both are, the current node is the answer. Rebuilding a tree uses in order plus pre order with an index map. And if they insist on constant extra space, Morris traversal threads temporary links to predecessors."]], ["which-bst-technique", ["A binary search tree adds order, which gives you a fast walk. If you're looking for one value or its neighbour, walk down comparing at each node. For floor and ceiling, remember the best candidate as you go. If you need sorted order, an in order traversal gives it, and a stack of the left spine gives it lazily, which is how you build an iterator or find the k-th smallest. To validate a BST, pass lower and upper bounds down, not just compare with the parent, because a node deep in the left subtree must also be smaller than the root. Deleting a node with two children replaces it with its in order successor. And you can build a BST from a pre order list in linear time by passing an upper bound."]], ["which-heap-pattern", ["Heaps are for when you repeatedly need the minimum or maximum of a changing set. For the k largest elements, keep a min heap of size k; its root is the k-th largest. To merge k sorted lists, push the head of each list and always pop the smallest, then push the next element from the same list. For a running median, keep two heaps: a max heap for the lower half and a min heap for the upper half, with sizes differing by at most one. Greedy problems like connecting sticks at minimum cost always combine the two cheapest. Task scheduling picks the most frequent remaining task first. And if the data is sorted once and never changes, you don't need a heap at all; just sort, or use quickselect."]], ["which-greedy", ["Greedy is tempting, so the first question in this ladder is a warning: can you find a counterexample that breaks the local choice? If yes, use DP or search instead. For intervals, the sort key matters. To keep the maximum number of non overlapping intervals, sort by end time and keep each interval that starts after the last kept one ends. To merge intervals, sort by start. For the minimum number of meeting rooms, sweep sorted starts and ends, or keep a min heap of end times. Fractional knapsack sorts by value per weight, but that only works when you can take fractions. Jump game tracks the farthest reachable index. Problems with a constraint on both neighbours, like the candy problem, use two passes, left to right and right to left, and combine."]], ["which-bit-trick", ["Bit tricks are a small toolkit. To read or write one bit, build a mask by shifting one left by k, then use AND to test, OR to set, AND with the inverted mask to clear, and XOR to toggle. N AND n minus one clears the lowest set bit, which tests for powers of two and counts set bits quickly. If every element appears twice except one, XOR everything, because pairs cancel. With two single elements, XOR everything, then split the numbers by their lowest set bit, using x AND minus x. If elements appear three times except one, count each bit position modulo three. To list every subset of up to twenty items, loop over masks from zero to two to the n minus one. And XOR from one to n follows a pattern that repeats every four numbers."]], ["which-string-technique", ["For strings, a few shapes recur. Balanced brackets with only one bracket type don't even need a stack; a depth counter is enough. Parsing text into a number is a small state machine: skip spaces, read a sign, read digits, stop at anything else, and clamp before you overflow. Roman numerals subtract a symbol when it's smaller than the one after it. Questions over all substrings depend on what you measure. Palindromic substrings expand around each of the two n minus one centres. Substrings with exactly k distinct characters use the at most k minus at most k minus one trick. And per substring frequency questions fix a start, extend the end and update a small count array incrementally."]], ["which-string-tool", ["When you're searching for patterns, choose the tool by the constraints. One pattern in one text with an exact answer: KMP, or the Z function on the pattern, a separator, and the text. Many patterns or many substring equality checks: rolling hashes, either verifying matches or using two hashes to make collisions vanishingly unlikely. Questions about borders, periods and the longest prefix that's also a suffix come straight from KMP's failure array. The shortest palindrome you can make by adding characters at the front uses KMP on the string, a separator, and its reverse. Prefix matches at every index are exactly what the Z function computes."]], ["which-trie", ["Use a trie when keys share prefixes. For strings, a twenty six way node with an end flag answers exists and starts with. Add pass and end counters if you need to count words with a prefix or support erase. Checking that every prefix of a word is itself a word is just a walk where every node must be an end. Counting distinct substrings inserts every suffix and counts the new nodes created. For integers and XOR, use a binary trie from bit thirty down to zero, and greedily choose the opposite bit at each level to maximise XOR. If each query also has a limit, sort the queries by that limit and insert numbers as you sweep, answering offline."]], ["which-sort-when", ["You rarely implement sorting in an interview, but you must know the trade-offs. Insertion sort is great for tiny or nearly sorted inputs, linear in the best case and stable. Merge sort guarantees n log n and is stable, at the cost of linear extra memory. Quicksort is in place and fast on average, but quadratic in the worst case, which a random pivot makes unlikely. Counting and bucket sorts beat n log n when values are small integers, because they don't compare elements at all. Selection sort and bubble sort are mainly teaching tools, though selection sort makes the fewest swaps."]], ["which-string-primitive", ["These are the everyday string primitives. Compare a string with its mirror using two pointers from both ends. If only the multiset of characters matters, as with anagrams, use a count array of size twenty six or two hundred fifty six. Structure preserving mappings, like isomorphic strings, need a two way map so that two different characters never map to the same one. Rotation checks are elegant: a goal is a rotation of s if it appears inside s concatenated with itself. And longest common prefix is a vertical scan, column by column."]], ["which-template", ["Number problems split by what they're about. Questions about digits use a digit loop: take the last digit with modulo ten and drop it with integer division. That counts digits, reverses numbers and checks palindromes and Armstrong numbers. Questions about the factors of one number pair each divisor i with n over i, so you only loop up to the square root. Questions about all primes up to n use the sieve of Eratosthenes. Two numbers' common factors use Euclid's algorithm: the greatest common divisor of a and b equals that of b and a mod b, and the least common multiple is a divided by the gcd times b. Factorials are a running product, but watch for overflow."]], ["which-prime-tool", ["And finally primes. For a single number, trial division up to its square root. For every prime in a range whose ends are huge but whose width is small, say up to ten to the twelve with a width of a million, use a segmented sieve. For all numbers up to about ten million, the ordinary sieve. If you need to factorise many numbers, precompute the smallest prime factor of each number and keep dividing by it. And for counts over many ranges, a prefix count of primes turns each query into a subtraction."]], [null, ["That's the decision trees. You don't need to memorise them word for word. Read them a few times, and soon you'll ask these questions automatically when you see a new problem."]]], "pat": [[null, ["Now we go deep on the core patterns. Each card follows the same structure: how to recognise the pattern, the skeleton of the code, its complexity, the pitfalls, and the problems from your notes that use it. I'll explain the idea behind each one, because if you understand why a pattern works, you can rebuild the code from scratch under pressure."]], ["two-pointers-opposite-ends-write-pointer", ["Two pointers comes in two flavours. The first is opposite ends. Put one pointer at the start and one at the end of a sorted array. If the pair sums to the target, you're done. If the sum is too small, the only way to increase it is to move the left pointer right. If it's too big, move the right pointer left. Each step throws away one element for good, so the whole scan is linear after sorting. The second flavour is the write pointer, used for in place rearranging. One pointer reads every element, and the other marks where the next kept element should be written. That's how you move zeroes to the end or remove duplicates while keeping order. Watch the pitfalls. For three sum, sort first, fix one element, run two pointers on the rest, and skip duplicate values both for the fixed element and after each hit, or you'll report the same triple twice. And in the Dutch national flag partition, when you swap the middle element with the high end, don't advance the middle pointer, because the element you just swapped in hasn't been examined yet. The problems listed underneath, like rotating an array by k with three reversals, are all variations on these two ideas."]], ["sliding-window-variable", ["The variable sliding window is two pointers moving in the same direction. The right pointer adds one element at a time. Whenever the window becomes invalid, the left pointer moves right until it's valid again. Because each index enters the window once and leaves at most once, the total work is linear, even though there's a loop inside a loop. The key precondition is monotone validity: shrinking a window must never make it worse. That's why it works with positive numbers but breaks with negatives. Look at the problems here. Longest substring without repeating characters keeps the last seen index of each character and jumps the left pointer past the previous occurrence. Max consecutive ones with k flips allows at most k zeros in the window. Minimum window substring keeps a count of how many required characters are still missing, expands until that count hits zero, then shrinks as far as it can. And there's a clever one, longest repeating character replacement, where the window is valid while its length minus its most frequent character count is at most k. Remember the counting rule too: to count windows with at most k of something, add the window length at each step, because every window ending at the right pointer and starting anywhere inside is valid."]], ["prefix-sum-hashmap", ["Prefix sums turn a range question into a difference of two numbers. If prefix of i is the sum of everything up to i, then the sum of the subarray from j plus one to i is prefix i minus prefix j. So to count subarrays that sum to k, walk through the array keeping a running sum, and at each step ask: how many earlier prefixes equal the current sum minus k? A hash map from prefix value to count answers that in constant time. Two details matter. Seed the map with zero mapped to one, which represents the empty prefix, so subarrays starting at index zero are counted. And query before you update, so a subarray never pairs with itself. For the longest subarray with sum k, store the first index where each prefix appears and never overwrite it, because an earlier start gives a longer subarray. The same idea works with XOR instead of sum. The problems under this card include two sum with a one pass hash map, three sum and four sum with sorting, and Kadane's algorithm, which is the running maximum subarray: keep the best sum ending here, which is either this element alone or this element added to the previous best."]], ["binary-search-bounds-on-answer", ["Binary search is about invariants, not about midpoints. The cleanest template keeps a half open range and finds the first index where a condition becomes true. While low is less than high, look at the middle. If the condition holds there, the answer is the middle or to its left, so set high to the middle. Otherwise the answer is strictly to the right, so set low to the middle plus one. When they meet, that's your answer. Lower bound, upper bound, first and last occurrence, floor and ceiling are all this template with a different condition. Searching on the answer uses exactly the same loop, but the range is over possible answers and the condition is a feasibility check. For example, can we ship all packages in d days with capacity c? If c works, any bigger capacity works too, so the condition is monotone, and binary search finds the smallest c that works. For real valued answers, just run a hundred iterations instead of comparing floating point numbers. And for rotated arrays, at every midpoint at least one half is sorted, so you can always decide which side the target must be on."]], ["monotonic-stack-deque", ["A monotonic stack keeps indices whose values are always increasing, or always decreasing, from bottom to top. When a new element arrives and breaks that order, you pop elements off the top, and each popped element has just found its answer: the new element is its next smaller, or next greater, element. Every index is pushed once and popped once, so the whole thing is linear. Be careful with ties: popping on greater or equal finds the next strictly smaller element, while popping on strictly greater finds the next smaller or equal one. The famous applications are the next greater element, the stock span, the largest rectangle in a histogram, where you compute the area of a bar at the moment it's popped, and the sum of subarray minimums, where each element contributes its value times the number of subarrays in which it's the minimum. The deque version solves the sliding window maximum: keep indices in decreasing value order, pop from the front when they leave the window, pop smaller ones from the back when a new element arrives, and the front is always the maximum. Trapping rain water also appears here, solved with two pointers by always moving the side with the lower wall."]], ["heap-priority-queue", ["A heap gives you the smallest element in constant time and lets you insert or remove in logarithmic time. The classic trick is a min heap of size k to find the k largest elements: push every element, and whenever the heap grows beyond k, pop the smallest. What remains are the k largest, and the root is the k-th largest. That costs n log k, which beats sorting when k is small. Python only has a min heap, so for a max heap you push negated values. Other patterns: merging k sorted lists by pushing a tuple of value, list index and element index; the running median with two balanced heaps; and the task scheduler, which has a neat counting formula. If the most frequent task appears f times and the cooldown is n, you need at least f minus one blocks of n plus one slots, plus one slot for every task that ties for the top frequency, and never less than the total number of tasks. For a single k-th largest query, quickselect is even faster: linear on average."]], ["linked-list-dummy-fast-slow-reverse", ["Linked list problems reward careful pointer handling. The dummy head is a fake node placed before the real head, so deleting or inserting at the front looks exactly like doing it in the middle. Fast and slow pointers move at speeds two and one. In a list with a cycle they must eventually meet, and here's the beautiful part: if you then move one pointer back to the head and advance both one step at a time, they meet exactly at the start of the cycle. That falls out of the distances: the distance from the head to the cycle start equals the distance from the meeting point forward to the cycle start, plus some whole number of laps. For reversal, keep three pointers: previous, current and next, and always save the next node before you redirect the current one's link. The problems listed here are the basic operations of insertion and deletion at the head, tail and k-th position, which you should be able to write without thinking."]], ["tree-dfs-return-value-global-max", ["This is the most important tree pattern. Write a recursive function that returns what the parent needs, and update a global answer with things the parent can't use. Diameter is the perfect example. The function returns the height of a subtree, because a path coming up from a child can only continue through one branch. But at each node, the longest path passing through that node uses both branches, the left height plus the right height, so you update the global best with that sum. Maximum path sum is the same shape: return the node's value plus the better child gain, ignoring negative gains, and update the global answer with the value plus both positive gains. The balanced tree check returns minus one as soon as any subtree is unbalanced, so you avoid recomputing heights. Lowest common ancestor returns a node if found, and if both children return something, the current node is the answer. The problems under this card also cover iterative traversals, which interviewers ask for when they want to see you manage a stack yourself."]], ["bst-walk-inorder", ["A binary search tree means left is smaller and right is larger at every node. So searching is a walk down one path, which takes time proportional to the height. Floor is the largest value not exceeding x: every time you step right from a node, that node is a candidate, so remember it. Ceiling is the mirror image. In order traversal of a BST gives sorted order, which solves the k-th smallest and validation. But validate with bounds: pass a lower and an upper limit down the recursion, because comparing a node only with its parent misses a node deep on the left that's bigger than the root. Deleting a node with two children copies its in order successor, the leftmost node of its right subtree, and then deletes that successor. For two sum on a BST, use two iterators, one walking forward and one backward, just like two pointers on a sorted array."]], ["trie-dict-shortcut", ["In Python you can build a trie in four lines with nested dictionaries: for each word, walk from the root, creating child dictionaries with set default, and mark the end with a special key. Each operation costs time proportional to the word length, regardless of how many words are stored. The listed problems extend it. Count and erase keeps a pass counter on every node and an end counter at the last node. Complete string checks that every prefix of a word is itself a word. Distinct substrings inserts every suffix and counts the nodes created. And the binary trie solves maximum XOR: insert numbers bit by bit from the most significant bit, then for each query walk down choosing the opposite bit whenever it exists, because a one in a high position beats anything in the lower positions."]], ["greedy-intervals", ["Greedy algorithms make the locally best choice and never look back, so they need a proof, usually an exchange argument: if an optimal solution made a different choice, you could swap in the greedy choice without making it worse. Intervals are the classic greedy family. To keep the maximum number of non overlapping intervals, sort by end time. Picking the interval that finishes earliest leaves the most room for the rest. So keep an interval whenever its start is at or after the last kept end, and the number to remove is n minus the number kept. Other problems here: assigning cookies by sorting both lists, giving change by always using the largest bill first, fractional knapsack by value per weight, jump game by tracking the farthest reach, and job sequencing by deadline with a min heap of the best jobs so far."]], ["bit-manipulation", ["Bits are worth knowing because the tricks are short and interviewers enjoy them. N AND n minus one clears the lowest set bit. So a number is a power of two exactly when that gives zero, and repeating it until zero counts the set bits, which is Brian Kernighan's method. X AND minus x isolates the lowest set bit. Looping a mask from zero to two to the n enumerates every subset. XOR has three properties that do all the work: x XOR x is zero, x XOR zero is x, and the order doesn't matter. So XORing everything cancels pairs and leaves the single number. The minimum bit flips to turn one number into another is just the number of set bits in their XOR."]]], "tpl": [[null, ["This section has sixteen code templates, each in Python and Java. You can switch the language on any card. I won't read code aloud, because that's not how anyone learns. Instead I'll tell you what each template is for, the invariant it maintains, and the one line that people usually get wrong."]], ["binary-search-lower-bound-and-search-on-the-answ", ["The first template is lower bound: the first index whose value is at least x. The range is half open, from zero to the length, so the answer can be the length itself, meaning every element is smaller. The second function, min feasible, is the same loop applied to answers instead of indices: it returns the smallest value for which a monotone check returns true. In Java, compute the middle as low plus half of high minus low, to avoid overflow. If you master just this one template, you can solve almost every binary search question."]], ["two-pointers-on-a-sorted-array", ["The pair with sum template moves inward from both ends. The comments say it all: if the sum is too small, move left to get a bigger sum; if it's too big, move right to get a smaller one. It returns the pair of indices or nothing. This is the inner loop of three sum and four sum."]], ["sliding-window-variable-size", ["This template finds the longest substring with at most k distinct characters. It keeps a count of characters in the window. After adding each character, while the number of distinct keys exceeds k, it removes the leftmost character and deletes keys whose count drops to zero. Then it records the window length. The one line people forget is deleting the zero count keys, which makes the distinct count wrong."]], ["prefix-sum-hash-map", ["Subarray sum equals k. Notice the comment on the first line: the map starts with the empty prefix. Then for each element, add it to the running total, add the number of earlier prefixes equal to total minus k, and only then record the current total. This order, query before update, matters when k is zero."]], ["monotonic-stack-next-greater-element", ["Next greater element. The stack holds indices whose values are decreasing. When a bigger value arrives, it is the answer for everything it beats, so pop them and record it. Anything left on the stack at the end has no greater element, which is why the result starts filled with minus one."]], ["heap-top-k-largest", ["Top k largest with a min heap of size k. Push each element, pop when the size exceeds k, and finally sort what's left in descending order. In Java the priority queue is a min heap by default, which is exactly what we want here."]], ["bfs-shortest-path-on-a-grid", ["Breadth first search on a grid. The queue processes cells in order of distance, so the first time you reach the goal, that distance is the shortest. The crucial comment says: mark when enqueued. If you mark a cell only when you pop it, the same cell can be added many times, which wastes time and can even give wrong counts. For several starting points, put all of them in the queue at distance zero, and the same code computes the distance to the nearest source."]], ["topological-sort-kahn-s-algorithm", ["Kahn's algorithm orders tasks so that every prerequisite comes first. Count incoming edges for each node, start with all nodes that have none, and each time you output a node, reduce the count of its neighbours, adding any that drop to zero. If the output has fewer nodes than the graph, some nodes never reached zero, which means there's a cycle. That's how course schedule problems detect impossible plans."]], ["dijkstra-s-shortest-paths", ["Dijkstra finds shortest paths when weights are non negative. Pop the closest unfinished node from a min heap, and relax its edges: if going through it gives a shorter path to a neighbour, update the distance and push the neighbour. The line people forget is skipping stale entries: when you pop a distance larger than the best known one, ignore it. Without that line the algorithm is still correct but can become much slower."]], ["union-find-disjoint-set-union", ["Union find tracks which items are in the same group. Each set is a tree, and find walks up to the root. Path halving makes each node point to its grandparent as it walks, which flattens the tree. Union by size attaches the smaller tree under the larger one. Together they make operations almost constant time. Union returns false when the two items were already connected, which is exactly how you detect a cycle in an undirected graph or skip an edge in Kruskal's algorithm."]], ["trie", ["The trie template, with insert, search and starts with. Both search and starts with walk the same path; the only difference is that search also requires the end of word marker. In Java, each node holds an array of twenty six children."]], ["backtracking-combination-sum", ["Backtracking for combination sum, where each candidate can be reused. Sort the candidates so you can stop the loop as soon as one is too big; that's the pruning. Choose a candidate, recurse with the same start index because reuse is allowed, then undo the choice by popping. When the remaining target hits zero, save a copy of the path. If each candidate could be used only once, you'd recurse with i plus one instead."]], ["dp-take-or-skip-and-0-1-knapsack", ["Two dynamic programming templates. House robber keeps just two numbers: the best total if we take the current house, and the best if we skip it. Taking it means we skipped the previous one. Then the zero one knapsack in one dimension, where dp of c is the best value with capacity c. The comment explains the most common DP mistake: loop the capacity backwards, so each item is used at most once. Looping forwards would let the same item be added again, which is the unbounded version."]], ["dp-on-two-strings-longest-common-subsequence", ["Longest common subsequence. Dp of i and j is the answer for the first i characters of one string and the first j of the other. If the last characters match, extend the diagonal by one. Otherwise take the better of dropping a character from either string. The extra row and column of zeros represent empty strings, which is why the code compares characters at i minus one and j minus one. Edit distance and wildcard matching use exactly the same grid."]], ["linked-list-reverse-and-find-the-middle", ["Reverse and middle, the two building blocks for palindrome lists, reorder list and reversing in k groups. Reversal saves the next node, points the current node backwards, and advances. The middle function returns the second middle node for even lengths, which is usually what you want when splitting a list in two."]], ["tree-dfs-return-a-value-update-a-global", ["The diameter template again, in code form. The helper returns the height, and the outer variable holds the best path through any node, measured in edges. In Python you need the nonlocal keyword to update it."]], ["difference-array-and-2d-prefix-sums", ["Two templates for ranges. A difference array handles many range additions efficiently: add v at the left index and subtract it just after the right index, then a single running sum at the end produces the final array. The two dimensional prefix sum precomputes rectangle sums, so any rectangle query becomes four lookups: the big rectangle, minus the one above, minus the one to the left, plus the corner that was subtracted twice. That's the inclusion exclusion principle in action. Padding with an extra zero row and column removes all the boundary checks."]]], "core": [[null, ["These tables come from your Striver notes. Each row is a pattern and its key insight in a few words. Let me add the reasoning that makes each insight stick."]], ["arrays-hashing", ["Dutch national flag uses three pointers: everything before low is zero, everything after high is two, and middle scans. Boyer Moore majority voting keeps a candidate and a count, incrementing on matches and decrementing otherwise; a true majority survives because it outnumbers everything else combined. For elements appearing more than n over three times, keep two candidates and verify them with a second pass. Kadane resets the running sum when it goes negative, because a negative prefix can only hurt. Next permutation finds the first dip from the right, swaps it with the next larger element to its right, and reverses the suffix. Counting inversions happens during merge sort: when you take an element from the right half, it's smaller than every remaining element in the left half. For the longest subarray with sum k, seed the prefix map with zero mapped to minus one. And longest consecutive sequence puts everything in a set and only starts counting from numbers whose predecessor isn't in the set, which keeps the whole thing linear."]], ["binary-search-shapes", ["Here are the binary search shapes. Lower bound finds the first element at least x; upper bound the first element strictly greater. Rotated arrays always have one sorted half. Minimum of maximum problems, like shipping packages, splitting an array, or painters partition, search between the largest element and the total sum with a greedy feasibility check. Maximum of minimum, like aggressive cows, places items greedily and searches the gap. Koko eating bananas searches over eating speed using ceiling division. The median of two sorted arrays partitions the smaller array so that everything on the left is no bigger than everything on the right. And the k-th missing positive uses the fact that a of i minus i minus one tells you how many numbers are missing before index i."]], ["sliding-window-shapes", ["The window shapes summarise what we discussed. Fixed size: add on the right, drop at i minus k. Longest valid: expand, and shrink while invalid. Exactly k distinct: at most k minus at most k minus one. Character replacement: valid while length minus max frequency is at most k, and the max frequency never needs to decrease, which is a subtle trick that keeps it linear. Minimum window substring: track how many characters are still needed. And if negatives are present, the window breaks, so use prefix sums or a monotonic deque."]], ["monotonic-stack-deque", ["This table tells you which order each stack keeps. Next greater element keeps a decreasing stack. Previous smaller keeps an increasing one. The histogram keeps an increasing stack and computes the area of a bar when it's popped, using the current index and the new top as its boundaries. Maximal rectangle in a matrix builds a histogram for each row. Remove k digits keeps an increasing stack and pops larger digits while it still has removals left, because a smaller digit earlier always makes a smaller number."]], ["linked-lists", ["The linked list insights. Dummy heads whenever the head may change. Fast and slow for middles and cycles. Reverse in place with three pointers, and reverse in k groups by reversing segments and reconnecting them. Intersection by switching heads at the end, because a plus b equals b plus a. Copying a list with random pointers in constant space by interleaving clones next to originals, setting the random pointers, then splitting. And the LRU cache, which is a hash map pointing into a doubly linked list with dummy head and tail nodes."]], ["heaps-greedy", ["Heaps and greedy, in one line each. K-th largest with a min heap of size k. Merging k sorted lists with a heap of tuples. Median of a stream with two balanced heaps. The task scheduler formula. Top k frequent elements with a counter and a heap, or bucket sort by frequency for linear time. Meeting rooms by sorting starts and keeping a min heap of end times; the heap size is the number of rooms. Gas station: if total gas covers total cost, the answer is the station after the last point where the running balance went negative. And candy with two passes."]], ["matrix-and-grid-techniques", ["Matrix and grid techniques are their own little world. Spiral order keeps four boundaries and shrinks one after walking each edge, rechecking the boundaries before the last two edges so a single row or column isn't printed twice. Rotating a square matrix ninety degrees clockwise is a transpose followed by reversing each row. Set matrix zeroes uses the first row and column as marker arrays, with two extra flags for themselves, which brings the space down to constant. A fully sorted matrix can be treated as one long array; a matrix sorted by rows and columns is searched with a staircase from the top right, moving left or down. Cells on the same diagonal share i minus j. Islands and flood fill are BFS or DFS over neighbours, marking on entry, and on very large grids you should prefer an explicit stack to avoid recursion limits. Shortest paths in grids are BFS for unit costs, zero one BFS for zero or one costs, and Dijkstra otherwise. And grid DP needs only one rolling row."]]], "tg": [[null, ["This section covers trees, graphs, tries, strings and bits in compact tables. Let me connect each row to the reasoning behind it."]], ["binary-trees-bst", ["Binary trees and BSTs. Diameter: the height function returns one arm, while the global uses both. Maximum path sum: return the value plus the best non negative arm. Lowest common ancestor in a binary tree: if the root is one of the targets, return it, and if both sides return something, the root is the answer. In a BST it's simpler: walk left while both are smaller, right while both are larger, and stop where they split. The views of a tree, top, bottom, left and right, are BFS with column or level bookkeeping. Serialise with null markers in pre order, so the shape is recoverable. Build from pre order and in order using a map from value to in order index. Burning a tree, or nodes at distance k, needs parent pointers so you can move upward. Morris traversal achieves constant space by threading. Path sum three, where a path can start anywhere, uses prefix sums along the current root path in a hash map, adding on the way down and removing on the way back. And recovering two swapped BST nodes uses an in order walk, where the inversions name the culprits."]], ["graphs", ["The graph table is a compressed version of the whole graph section. BFS for unweighted shortest paths and multi source spreading, like rotting oranges. DFS for components and cycles: in undirected graphs track the parent, in directed graphs track the nodes on the current path. Kahn for topological order and cycle detection. Dijkstra with a priority queue and stale entry skipping, only for non negative weights. Bellman Ford relaxes all edges V minus one times, and if a V-th round still improves something, there's a negative cycle. Floyd Warshall for all pairs, with the intermediate node loop outermost. Prim and Kruskal for minimum spanning trees. Tarjan's low link values for bridges and articulation points. Kosaraju for strongly connected components. And zero one BFS with a deque, pushing zero weight edges to the front."]], ["tries-strings-bits-maths", ["Tries, strings, bits and maths. A trie with an end flag and counts. Max XOR with a bit trie. KMP builds the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix for every position, and matches in linear time. The Z function computes, for each index, how long a prefix of the string matches starting there; to search, build it on the pattern, a separator and the text. Rabin Karp uses a rolling hash and verifies on a hit. Longest palindromic substring expands around centres, or uses Manacher's algorithm for linear time. XOR from one to n cycles with period four. The lowest set bit and clearing it we've seen. Submask enumeration uses s equals s minus one AND mask. The sieve starts marking at p squared. And fast power uses binary exponentiation, squaring the base and halving the exponent."]], ["beyond-the-sheet-triggers", ["These triggers take you beyond the standard sheet. Range sum with point updates: a Fenwick tree. Range updates with range queries: a lazy segment tree. Static range minimum with many queries: a sparse table. Counting numbers in a range with a digit property: digit DP. An answer for every possible root: rerooting DP. Many k-th ancestor or LCA queries: binary lifting. Subset sums with n around forty: meet in the middle. And sampling uniformly from a stream of unknown length: reservoir sampling."]], ["graph-picker", ["The graph picker is a quick decision helper. No weights: BFS. Weights of zero or one: zero one BFS. Non negative weights: Dijkstra. Negative weights: Bellman Ford. All pairs on a small graph: Floyd Warshall. Ordering or dependencies: topological sort. Connecting everything cheaply: Kruskal with union find."]], ["randomised-algorithms", ["Randomised algorithms appear less often, but they're elegant. Reservoir sampling keeps the first k items, and for the i-th item replaces a random slot with probability k over i, which gives every item an equal chance even though you never know the stream's length. Fisher Yates shuffles in place by swapping each position with a random earlier or equal position, walking backwards. Weighted random picks build prefix sums of the weights, draw a random number, and binary search for it. Randomised quickselect finds the k-th element in expected linear time. Rejection sampling builds a bigger uniform range and throws away values outside the target, like making rand ten from rand seven. Random bases defeat adversarial hash collisions. And skip lists promote each node to the next level with probability one half, giving a sorted structure with expected logarithmic operations, which is what Redis uses for sorted sets."]], ["maths-toolkit-modular-arithmetic-combinatorics-g", ["The maths toolkit. Reduce modulo m after every addition and multiplication to avoid overflow. Division modulo a prime p is multiplication by the inverse, which by Fermat's little theorem is b to the power p minus two. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials, and n choose r becomes three multiplications. Catalan numbers count BST shapes and valid parenthesis strings. Fibonacci in logarithmic time uses matrix exponentiation. For geometry, the cross product tells you whether a turn is counter clockwise, the shoelace formula gives a polygon's area, and you should compare squared distances to avoid square roots and floating point error. Rotating coordinates by forty five degrees turns Manhattan distance into Chebyshev distance. And for counting, inclusion exclusion handles \"divisible by a or b\", while the pigeonhole principle guarantees duplicates."]]], "graph": [[null, ["Graphs are where many candidates freeze, so let's slow down. A graph is just things and connections. Cities and roads, courses and prerequisites, people and friendships, cells in a grid and their neighbours. Once you see the graph, the question becomes: what am I looking for, are the edges directed or weighted, and could the graph be disconnected?"]], ["which-graph-algorithm-your-27-graphs-readme", ["This diagram from your graphs readme is the master selector. If you only need reachability, components or flood fill, BFS or DFS will do, and union find is better if edges arrive over time. For cycles, it depends on direction. In an undirected graph, a DFS that ignores the edge back to its parent, or union find, detects a cycle. In a directed graph, you need to know whether a node is on the current path, or you can run Kahn's algorithm and see if fewer than V nodes get processed. For ordering with dependencies, topological sort. For shortest paths, the weights decide: all equal means BFS; zero or one means a deque based BFS; a DAG with any weights means relaxing edges in topological order; non negative means Dijkstra; negative means Bellman Ford; and all pairs on a few hundred nodes means Floyd Warshall. To connect everything cheaply, build a minimum spanning tree with Kruskal or Prim. And for critical edges, cut vertices or strongly connected components, Tarjan's low link or Kosaraju."]], ["goal-algorithm-practice-graph-md", ["This table pairs each goal with the detail that makes the algorithm correct. BFS: mark visited when you enqueue, not when you dequeue. Dijkstra: skip stale heap entries. Bellman Ford: copy the distance array each round when the number of edges matters, like at most k stops. Components: count how many times your outer loop starts a new search. Directed cycles: three states, where state one means on the current stack. Bipartite: colour with BFS and look for a contradiction. Closest source: seed every source at distance zero. Can X reach Y: search backwards from Y, like the Pacific Atlantic problem, where you flood inwards from each ocean. And the three questions to ask first: what am I looking for, is it directed or weighted, and could it be disconnected so I need an outer loop."]], ["bfs-mark-on-enqueue", ["The BFS skeleton. Start with the source in both the visited set and the queue. Pop a node, look at its neighbours, and for each unvisited neighbour mark it and enqueue it immediately. Because BFS explores in rings of increasing distance, the first time it reaches a node is along a shortest path."]], ["dfs-components-outer-loop-mandatory", ["Counting components. The outer loop goes over every node, and whenever it finds an unvisited one, it starts a DFS and increments the count. The comment says the outer loop is mandatory, because a single search from node zero only sees one component."]], ["cycle-directed-3-state-dfs", ["Directed cycle detection with three states. Zero means unvisited, one means currently on the recursion stack, two means fully explored. If you reach a node in state one, you've found a back edge, so there's a cycle. A node in state two is safe, because everything reachable from it has already been checked. That's why parent tracking, which works for undirected graphs, is wrong here."]], ["topo-sort-kahn", ["The compact Kahn's algorithm. Queue every node with no incoming edges, pop, append to the order, decrement neighbours, enqueue those that hit zero. If the order is shorter than n, return empty to signal a cycle."]], ["dijkstra", ["Compact Dijkstra. The stale check is the line that says: if the popped distance is greater than the recorded distance, continue. Remember that Dijkstra is greedy: once a node is popped with its final distance, nothing can improve it, and that's only true when no edge is negative."]], ["bellman-ford-k-stops", ["Bellman Ford relaxes every edge in rounds. After round r, every shortest path that uses at most r edges is correct. That's why it solves cheapest flights with at most k stops: run k plus one rounds. But you must relax from a copy of the previous round's distances, otherwise one round could chain several edges together and break the limit. If one more round after V minus one still improves a distance, there's a negative cycle."]], ["floyd-warshall", ["Floyd Warshall computes all pairs shortest paths with three nested loops, and the intermediate node k must be the outermost loop. The meaning is: after processing k, every distance is the shortest using only the first k nodes as stepping stones. It's cubic, so it's for a few hundred nodes at most. A negative value on the diagonal reveals a negative cycle."]], ["prim-mst", ["Prim's algorithm grows one tree. Start from any node and keep a min heap of edges leaving the tree; repeatedly take the cheapest edge that reaches a new node. If you finish with fewer than n nodes, the graph wasn't connected."]], ["dsu-kruskal", ["Kruskal's algorithm sorts all edges by weight and adds each edge unless it would create a cycle, which union find detects in almost constant time. Note the comment about rank: the rank array starts at zero for every node, and the parent array starts as each node pointing to itself. A classic bug is initialising the parent array to all zeros, which puts everything in one set."]], ["tarjan-bridges-low-link", ["Tarjan's bridge finding gives each node a discovery time and a low link value: the earliest discovery time reachable from its subtree using at most one back edge. An edge from u to child v is a bridge when the low value of v is greater than the discovery time of u, meaning v's subtree has no other way back up. For articulation points the condition is greater than or equal, with a special rule for the root: it's a cut vertex only if it has at least two DFS children."]], ["kosaraju-scc", ["Kosaraju finds strongly connected components, groups where every node can reach every other. First, DFS the graph and record nodes in order of finishing. Then reverse every edge and DFS again, taking nodes in reverse finishing order. Each search in the second pass discovers exactly one component."]], ["0-1-bfs-multi-source", ["Zero one BFS uses a deque instead of a priority queue. When an edge costs zero, push the neighbour to the front, because it's just as close; when it costs one, push to the back. This gives Dijkstra's answer in linear time. And multi source BFS seeds every source at distance zero, so the distances you compute are to the nearest source."]], ["common-mistakes-practice-graph-md-dsu-md", ["Read this list of common mistakes slowly, because each one has cost someone an interview. Forgetting disconnected graphs. Marking visited on dequeue. Using parent tracking on a directed graph. Dijkstra without the stale check. Topological sort without checking that every node was output. Bellman Ford updating in place when edges are limited. A union find with the parent array set to zeros, or that forgets to return false when the two nodes are already joined. Using DFS for shortest paths in an unweighted graph, which is simply wrong, because DFS doesn't explore by distance. Using Dijkstra with negative edges. Recursing a hundred thousand levels deep in Python, which crashes. Building an undirected graph with only one direction of each edge. And union find without path compression, which degrades to linear time."]], ["problems-to-know-cold-practice-graph-md", ["These are the graph problems to know cold. Number of islands is grid DFS. Clone graph is DFS with a map from old node to new node. Pacific Atlantic is reverse search from the borders. Course schedule one and two are topological sort. Word ladder is BFS where neighbours are words one letter apart. Rotting oranges is multi source BFS. Network delay time is Dijkstra. Cheapest flights within k stops is Bellman Ford with a round limit. Redundant connection is union find. Is graph bipartite is BFS two colouring. Alien dictionary is topological sort on characters. And accounts merge is union find over emails. If you can explain each one in two sentences, you're in good shape."]]], "dp3": [[null, ["Dynamic programming has a reputation for being hard, but it's really one idea: if the same subproblem appears again and again, solve it once and remember the answer. Everything else is technique. This section goes through every family in depth."]], ["the-framework-4-questions-practice-dp-md", ["Here's the framework, four questions. First, the state: what does dp of i mean, as an exact English sentence? If you can't say it in a sentence, you're not ready to write code. Second, the recurrence: how does dp of i depend on smaller states? Write the maths before the code. Third, base cases, derived from the definition, not guessed. For example, the number of ways to make zero is one, because doing nothing is one way. Fourth, where's the answer: dp of n, the maximum over all dp values, or dp of zero and n minus one for interval problems. Then the path to an optimal solution is always the same: write the recursion, add memoisation for top down, flip the direction for bottom up tabulation, and finally keep only the rows you need to save space. The diagram on this card shows how the state's index tells you the family: a position in one array, a grid cell, an index plus remaining capacity, a day plus holding state, an index plus previous choice, two string positions, or an interval with a split point."]], ["signal-pattern-practice-dp-md-decision-table", ["This decision table maps problem signals to DP families. Choices on a one dimensional array that affect neighbours: linear DP. Moving right and down in a grid: grid DP. Aligning two strings: two sequence DP. Take or leave with a capacity: knapsack. Longest increasing chain: the LIS family. Burst, cut or merge a contiguous range: interval DP, filled by length. A node needing its children first: tree DP. Buying and selling with rules: the stock state machine. Up to twenty items with subsets as state: bitmask DP. Counting numbers up to N with a digit property: digit DP. And the most common mistake gets its own note: in one dimensional knapsack, loop backwards for items used at most once, forwards for unlimited items. For coin change two, which counts combinations, the coins loop must be outside, otherwise you count each ordering separately, which gives permutations."]], ["every-dp-family-state-transition-base-fill-order", ["This big table gives each family's state, transition, base case, fill order and answer. Let me highlight what's subtle in each. Linear DP like climbing stairs and house robber needs only the previous two values, so constant space. Grid DP fills row by row, with blocked cells set to zero, and one rolling row is enough. The stock state machine tracks the best cash when holding and when not holding, starting with holding at minus infinity, because you can't hold stock before buying. Zero one knapsack loops capacity in descending order; unbounded in ascending order. In the LIS family, dp of i is the longest increasing subsequence ending at i, which is quadratic, or you use the tails array with binary search for n log n, remembering that tails is not itself the subsequence. Two string DP aligns s one of i minus one with dp of i, because row zero represents the empty string. Interval DP must be filled by length, so that smaller intervals are ready before bigger ones. Tree DP returns a pair from each node, like the best with and without the node. And bitmask DP keys on the set of visited items and the last item."]], ["1d-linear-your-problems", ["Your one dimensional problems: climbing stairs, frog jump, frog jump with k distances, maximum sum of non adjacent elements, house robber, and ninja's training. They're all the same skeleton: dp of i built from a few earlier values. House robber two, where houses are in a circle, runs the linear version twice: once without the first house and once without the last. Ninja's training adds a small state for which activity was done yesterday, so you never repeat it on consecutive days."]], ["2d-grid-your-problems", ["Grid problems. Unique paths adds the count from above and from the left. Unique paths two zeroes out blocked cells. Minimum falling path sum takes the best of the three cells above. Triangle works bottom up, which avoids special cases at the edges. Cherry pickup two moves two robots down simultaneously, so the state is the row and both columns. Count square submatrices of ones uses a lovely recurrence: the largest square ending at a cell is one plus the minimum of the three neighbours above, left and diagonal. And minimum path sum uses one rolling row."]], ["stock-state-machine-your-problems", ["The stock problems all model your position. With one transaction, track the minimum price so far and the best profit. With unlimited transactions, a two state machine of holding and not holding. With two or k transactions, keep a buy and sell value for each transaction. A transaction fee subtracts on selling. And a cooldown means you can only buy two days after selling, which needs one extra state or a look ahead of two days. Drawing the states as circles with arrows labelled buy, sell and rest makes these problems almost mechanical."]], ["0-1-knapsack-subset-your-problems", ["The zero one knapsack family. Subset sum asks whether some subset reaches a target, a boolean DP. Partition equal subset sum checks whether half the total is reachable. Minimum subset sum difference fills the boolean table up to half the total and picks the largest reachable sum. Count subsets with sum k counts instead of checking. Count partitions with a given difference reduces to counting subsets with sum total plus d over two. Target sum, where you put plus or minus signs, is the same reduction. All of these loop the target backwards in one dimension."]], ["unbounded-knapsack-your-problems", ["Unbounded knapsack. Minimum coins loops the amount upward, taking one plus the best for amount minus the coin. Coin change two counts combinations with coins in the outer loop. Rod cutting treats lengths as items that can be reused. The upward loop is what allows reuse."]], ["lis-family-your-problems", ["The LIS family. The n log n method keeps a tails array, where tails of k is the smallest possible tail of an increasing subsequence of length k plus one, and each new number either extends the array or replaces the first tail that's at least as big. To print the actual subsequence, use the quadratic DP with a parent array. Largest divisible subset sorts first and uses divisibility instead of less than. Longest string chain sorts words by length and tries deleting each character. Longest bitonic subsequence adds an increasing pass and a decreasing pass. And the number of longest increasing subsequences keeps a count array alongside the lengths."]], ["two-strings-lcs-edit-your-problems", ["Two string DP. Longest common subsequence we've seen. Longest common substring resets to zero on a mismatch, because a substring must be contiguous. Longest palindromic subsequence is the LCS of the string and its reverse. Minimum insertions to make a palindrome is n minus that. Shortest common supersequence walks back through the LCS table. Distinct subsequences counts how many ways one string appears as a subsequence of another. Edit distance takes one plus the minimum of insert, delete and replace on a mismatch. And wildcard matching handles a star by either matching nothing or consuming one more character."]], ["interval-partition-mcm-your-problems", ["Interval DP, also called matrix chain multiplication style. The state is an interval, and the transition tries every split point. Minimum cost to cut a stick sorts the cut positions and adds the stick length at each split. Burst balloons is famous for a twist: instead of choosing which balloon to burst first, you choose which balloon bursts last in the interval, because then its neighbours are known. Evaluate boolean expression counts true and false ways for each interval. And palindrome partitioning two cuts the string at the minimum number of places so each piece is a palindrome."]], ["tree-bitmask-graph-dp-dp-patterns", ["Tree, bitmask and graph DP. Diameter and maximum path sum we've covered. Binary tree cameras uses three states for each node: needs a camera, has one, or is covered. Distributing coins returns each subtree's surplus or deficit, and the moves add up the absolute values. House robber three returns two numbers per node: rob it or skip it. The travelling salesman problem uses dp over a mask of visited cities and the last city. Partition into k equal subsets uses a mask with a running remainder. And the longest path in a DAG relaxes edges in topological order."]], ["0-1-knapsack-recursion-tree-dp-patterns", ["This little recursion tree shows the knapsack choice at each item: exclude it and keep the capacity, or include it and reduce the capacity. Each path from root to leaf is one subset, and the repeated subtrees are what memoisation saves."]], ["fibonacci-overlap-memo-dp-patterns", ["And this is the picture that explains dynamic programming. Computing Fibonacci of four recursively computes Fibonacci of two twice and Fibonacci of one three times. The tree grows exponentially, but there are only n distinct subproblems. Store each answer the first time, and the exponential tree collapses into a line."]], ["stock-state-machine", ["Here's the stock state machine drawn out. From rest you can buy, paying the price, and move to hold. From hold you can sell, earning the price minus any fee, and move to sold. From sold, a cooldown day returns you to rest. You can also stay in rest or hold. Writing one DP variable per state and one transition per arrow gives you the code."]], ["common-dp-mistakes-practice-dp-md-striver", ["And the common DP mistakes. The wrong knapsack direction. Guessing base cases like dp of zero equals one or zero instead of deriving them. Thinking the tails array is the actual LIS. Filling interval DP by left or right index instead of by length. Forgetting that s one of i minus one aligns with dp of i. In maximum product subarray, tracking only the maximum, when a negative number can turn the minimum into the new maximum. In target sum, not checking that the total plus target is even and non negative. And in burst balloons, choosing the first balloon instead of the last."]]], "dp": [[null, ["This section looks at dynamic programming through eleven classic pattern folders. You'll recognise a lot from the previous section, so I'll focus on the map and the method."]], ["dp-family-map", ["The DP family map has nine areas. Zero one knapsack, with subset sum, equal partition, count subsets and target sum. Unbounded knapsack, with coin change, rod cutting, perfect squares and word break. The LCS family. The LIS family, including the Russian doll envelopes. Kadane's family, including circular arrays, maximum product, and the first three stock problems. Interval DP. Grid DP, including the dungeon game that's filled backwards and cherry pickup. Trees and graphs. And bitmask DP. When you meet a new DP problem, try to place it in one of these boxes first."]], ["4-step-method-recursion-dp", ["The four step method from recursion to DP: identify what changes, which becomes the state; list the choices; write the base cases; then memoise, tabulate and optimise space. The table on this card gives the key transition for each family. A few deserve emphasis. Russian doll envelopes sorts by width ascending and height descending, then runs LIS on heights; the descending order prevents two envelopes with the same width from nesting. Circular Kadane takes the larger of the normal maximum and the total minus the minimum subarray, unless every number is negative. The dungeon game is filled from the bottom right, because the health you need at a cell depends on what comes after it. Cherry pickup moves two walkers at once so they don't double count cherries. And bitmask TSP is two to the n times n squared."]], ["advanced-dp-templates", ["Advanced DP templates. Digit DP counts numbers up to N with some property by going digit by digit, tracking whether you're still tight against N's digits, whether you've started placing non zero digits, and whatever the property needs, like the digit sum. The example code counts numbers up to N whose digits sum to S. Rerooting computes an answer for every possible root with two passes: one from children upward, and one pushing the parent's side downward. DP on DAGs processes nodes in topological order. Expectation DP computes expected values as weighted sums of next states. And to reconstruct an answer, store the choice at each state and walk back from the end."]]], "rec": [[null, ["Recursion is the foundation under backtracking, trees and DP, so it's worth getting comfortable with it."]], ["which-recursion-shape-18-recursion-readme", ["There are three recursion shapes. Pick or not pick: for each element, either include it or not. That gives two to the n leaves and solves subsets and subset sums. The for loop shape: at each level, choose which element comes next, starting from an index so you don't revisit earlier ones. That's combinations and partitions. And place, check, recurse, undo: try a value in a cell, check the constraints, recurse, and undo if it fails, which is N queens, Sudoku and graph colouring. Then decide on the output. To collect all answers, copy the path at the leaves. To find any one, return true and stop at the first success. To count, return the sum of the branches. With duplicates in the input, sort and skip a value equal to the previous one at the same depth."]], ["how-to-solve-any-recursion-dp-patterns-recursion", ["How to solve any recursion. Define the base case, the simplest input you can answer directly. Make a recursive call on a smaller version of the same problem. Combine the results. And trust the recursion: don't try to trace every level in your head. For DFS there are three questions. What am I carrying down: the path, the remaining target, the depth? What am I returning up: a value, a boolean, nothing? And do I need to undo anything when I come back? Then four rules that never change: base case first, mark visited before recursing, save a copy of the path, and every append has a matching pop."]], ["pick-not-pick-decision-tree-subsets-of-1-2", ["This tree shows pick or not pick for the subsets of one and two. At the first level you pick or skip one; at the second you pick or skip two. The four leaves are the four subsets: one and two, just one, just two, and the empty set."]], ["for-loop-combinations-decision-tree", ["The for loop shape draws a different tree. From the root you can start with any element, and each child only considers elements after the one just taken. That ordering is what prevents the same combination appearing twice in different orders."]], ["place-check-recurse-undo-n-queens", ["N queens shows place, check, recurse, undo. In row zero, try each column. If it's safe, place the queen and move to row one. If no column works in some row, undo the last placement and try the next column. When you place a queen in every row, record the board. Checking safety is fast with three sets: used columns, used diagonals by row minus column, and used anti diagonals by row plus column."]], ["roadmap-easy", ["The easy recursion roadmap: factorial, Fibonacci, sum of digits, power, reversing a string, palindromes, counting set bits, array sums, recursive binary search and string length. These build the habit of base case plus smaller call."]], ["roadmap-medium", ["The medium roadmap: subsets, permutations, Tower of Hanoi, combination sum, phone letter combinations, generating parentheses, subset sums, Josephus, atoi and tree traversals. These teach the decision tree shapes."]], ["roadmap-hard", ["And the hard roadmap: N queens, Sudoku, word search two, palindrome partitioning, wildcard and regex matching, flattening nested lists, the k-th permutation, rat in a maze and m colouring. These combine recursion with pruning, and some turn into DP once you notice repeated states."]]], "bt": [[null, ["This short section gathers the backtracking template, the classic gotchas, and the follow up questions interviewers like to chain."]], ["backtracking-template", ["The backtracking template records the current path at every node for subsets, loops from the start index, skips duplicates at the same depth, chooses, recurses and undoes. Then the variations in one line each. Permutations use a used array and loop from zero. N queens uses the three sets. Sudoku tries digits one to nine and validates the row, column and box. Word search marks a cell as used and restores it when backtracking."]], ["gotchas-patterns-master", ["Seven gotchas worth repeating. Negatives break sliding windows. Skip duplicates only at the same depth, which is why the condition is i greater than start. Tree DP returns one arm but the global uses both. Dijkstra skips stale entries. Binary search on the answer needs a monotone feasibility check, so prove that first. Greedy needs an exchange argument, otherwise use DP. And for intervals, merge sorts by start while counting non overlapping sorts by end."]], ["follow-up-chains", ["Interviewers rarely stop at one question; they chain follow ups. If you solve two sum, expect: what if the input is sorted, then three sum, then a BST, then a stream. Sliding window at most k leads to exactly k, then negatives. Binary search leads to rotated arrays, duplicates, matrices and searching on the answer. Tree DFS leads to iterative versions, returning the path, then constant space. Linear DP leads to space optimisation, printing the solution, then k transactions. Graph BFS leads to weighted edges, multiple sources, and bidirectional search. Knowing the chain lets you prepare the next answer while you finish the current one."]]], "ds": [[null, ["Now a reference section on data structure operations, which helps you justify complexity claims precisely."]], ["data-structure-operations", ["This table lists the cost of each operation. Arrays give constant access, constant append at the end, and linear insertion in the middle. Hash maps and sets give constant time on average for search, insert and delete. Stacks and queues are constant at their ends. Heaps give the minimum in constant time and insert or remove in logarithmic time. Linked lists insert in constant time if you already hold the node, but searching is linear. Balanced BSTs, which are TreeMap in Java or sorted containers in Python, give logarithmic everything plus ordering. Tries are proportional to the key length. Union find is almost constant. Segment trees and Fenwick trees are logarithmic for queries and updates. The Python and Java columns tell you which built in type to use, which saves time in the interview."]], ["fenwick-tree-practice-data-structures-11", ["The Fenwick tree, or binary indexed tree, stores partial sums so that both prefix sums and point updates take logarithmic time. The magic is in i AND minus i, the lowest set bit: an update climbs by adding it, a query descends by subtracting it. The range sum from l to r is the prefix up to r minus the prefix up to l minus one. Beyond sums, it handles range updates with point queries by storing differences, counts inversions by scanning right to left over compressed ranks, finds the k-th smallest by binary lifting, and extends to two dimensions."]], ["design-a-data-structure-problems", ["Design a data structure questions test whether you can combine simple pieces. A min stack stores each value with the minimum at the time it was pushed. A queue from two stacks pours from the in stack to the out stack only when the out stack is empty, which makes each operation amortised constant. An LRU cache combines a hash map with a doubly linked list. LFU adds frequency lists and a minimum frequency pointer. Insert, delete and get random in constant time uses an array plus a map from value to index, deleting by swapping with the last element. Hit counters use a circular buffer. A time based key value store binary searches timestamps. A median stream uses two heaps. Twitter's feed merges followees' recent tweets with a heap. And a hash map from scratch uses buckets and doubles the capacity when the load factor reaches about three quarters. For each, start by saying which operations must be fast, then pick structures that make each one fast."]]], "jp": [[null, ["If you code in both Java and Python, this section saves you from translation slips during the interview."]], ["java-python-for-dsa", ["This table lines up the same operation in both languages: counting with a map, default values, lists and arrays, stacks with ArrayDeque, queues, min and max heaps, sorting with a key versus a comparator, building strings with join versus StringBuilder, character access and substrings. Two lines deserve special attention. In Java, comparing strings with double equals compares references, not content, so always use equals. And integer division: five divided by two is two in both languages, but they disagree for negatives, as the next card explains."]], ["gotchas-that-cost-an-hour-each-java-python-refer", ["These gotchas each cost someone an hour. In Java: string comparison with double equals; double equals on Integer objects compares references, which only seems to work between minus one twenty eight and one twenty seven because those values are cached; List of creates an immutable list; array length has no parentheses while string length does; modifying a list inside a for each loop throws; Arrays as list on an int array gives a list of arrays; list remove with an int removes by index, not value; a comparator written as a minus b can overflow; and the absolute value of the minimum integer is still negative. In Python: sort returns None, so never assign it; a mutable default argument is shared across calls; multiplying a list of lists shares one row; heapq is a min heap only; and floor division of a negative number rounds down, so minus seven floor divided by two is minus four, while Java truncates toward zero and gives minus three."]]], "find": [[null, ["This is your practice engine: a filterable bank of four hundred and thirty four problems from your Striver notes."]], ["problem-bank-434-problems", ["I won't read the table, because it's meant to be used, not listened to. Here's how to use it well. Each row has the problem, the best approach in a few words, the time and space complexity, the most common mistake, and the interview angle, meaning what to say out loud. Filter by topic with the chips, or type a technique like monotonic or knapsack to see every problem that uses it. A good routine is to pick a topic, cover the approach column, and try to name the approach for each problem in ten seconds. Then read the common mistake column, because those are exactly the bugs you'll make under pressure. And the interview angle column gives you sentences that show your thinking, like saying the overflow boundary out loud before you write a loop."]]], "py": [[null, ["A quick section on Python essentials and complexity, which is useful the night before an interview."]], ["python-essentials", ["These are the imports you'll use in almost every Python solution: Counter, defaultdict and deque from collections; heapq and bisect; and cache from functools for memoisation. Max heaps by negating values. Bisect left is lower bound. Sorting with a tuple key handles multiple criteria. And raise the recursion limit for deep recursion. The table reminds you of the hidden costs: inserting or popping at the front of a list is linear, so use a deque."]], ["big-o-reference", ["The Big O reference. Sorting is n log n; quicksort is quadratic in the worst case. Counting sort is n plus k. BFS and DFS are V plus E. Dijkstra with a heap is V plus E times log V. Bellman Ford is V times E. Floyd is V cubed. Kruskal is E log E. Trie operations are proportional to the word length. And generating all subsets or permutations is two to the n times n, or n factorial times n, because each answer costs n to copy."]]], "last": [[null, ["We've reached the last section: the checklist for the final hour before an interview. Let me read it the way I'd say it to you outside the interview room."]], ["last-minute-revision-one-line-each", ["Read the constraints first and pick your target complexity from the table. Name the pattern out loud, give the brute force, then optimise. For prefix maps, the longest variant seeds zero with minus one, and the counting variant seeds zero with one. Exactly k equals at most k minus at most k minus one. Use binary search on the answer for minimum of maximum and maximum of minimum. Use a monotonic stack for next greater or smaller, and the contribution technique for sums of minimums. In tree DP, return one arm and update the global with both. In one dimensional knapsack, zero one goes descending and unbounded goes ascending. For Russian doll envelopes, sort width ascending and height descending, then run LIS. For burst balloons, choose the last balloon. And always dry run your edge cases: empty input, one element, all equal values, negatives and overflow."]], [null, ["That's the whole DSA sheet. If you've listened all the way through, go back and try a few problems from the bank in a topic that felt shaky. You've got this."]]]};
